		<published>2025-05-20T22:40:59</published>
		<id>http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=3355&amp;p=37177#p37177</id>
		<link href="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=3355&amp;p=37177#p37177"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Baofeng DM-1701 / Retevis RT-84 specific • Re: Hum (interference) in DM-1701]]></title>

					<category term="Baofeng DM-1701 / Retevis RT-84 specific" scheme="http://localhost/opengd77/viewforum.php?f=25" label="Baofeng DM-1701 / Retevis RT-84 specific"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=3355&amp;p=37177#p37177"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Hello all, I can also confirm the same issue with DM-1701 on FM transmissions. The correspondent hears a hum / buzz when the light is lower than 100%. <br>I also notice this issue in a parallel development for a different terminal, a Quansheng UV-K5. When i contacted the developer, Armel - F4HWN, <br>he said he solved the problem by changing the PWM frequency of the screen parameters. The PWM of the light driver frequency was low, and after he changed it to a higher frequency, the topic was solved. Maybe the developers in this forum can use this technical detail in their analysis. This is a constructive remark, I hope it can be used. Thanks to the developers for all the effort of bringing new features to OpenGD77 development. Great deal of respect. <br>73 de YO2AP, Giani</div></blockquote>AFIK F4HWN has not posted details of the changes they made to the PWM frequency.<br><br>FYI.<br>Different radios seem to need different PWM rates in order to minimise any interference to the Tx audio.<br><br>There is no simple solution which works for all radios, because using a higher frequency causes problems with the backlight brightness on some radios;  specifically the MD9600 / RT90 is very sensitive to PWM frequency and the optimum PWM frequency is different for each hardware version of this radio.<br><br>The DM1701 firmware build output is separate from the UV380/390 so it is possible to make a change specifically for this radio, however possibly there are different versions of DM1701 hardware which Baofeng does not publicise, and changing the PWM rate to fix a 'hum' problem on one users DM1701 may possibly cause the backlight to not work on another radio where the PWM components may not be identical <img class="smilies" src="http://localhost/opengd77/images/smilies/icon_e_sad.gif" width="15" height="17" alt=":-(" title="Sad"><p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Tue May 20, 2025 10:40 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
